\section*{Euclidean sharp filling bounds in proper CAT(0) spaces}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Sharp fillings in CAT(0)}: The paper claims the Euclidean-optimal integral filling inequality for compactly supported integer cycles of dimension at least two in proper CAT(0) spaces. Arbitrary integer multiplicities and ambient dimensions are permitted.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} For every proper CAT$(0)$ metric space $X$ and integer $n\ge2$, each compactly supported integral $n$-current $T$ with $\partial T=0$ is claimed to bound a compactly supported integral $(n+1)$-current $S$ with \[\partial S=T,\qquad\mathbf M(S)\le\frac{\mathbf M(T)^{(n+1)/n}}{(n+1)^{(n+1)/n}\omega_{n+1}^{1/n}}.\] No ambient-dimension or multiplicity restriction is imposed, and the constant is claimed optimal. Its manifold corollary gives $\operatorname{area}(\partial\Omega)\ge d\omega_d^{1/d}\operatorname{vol}(\Omega)^{(d-1)/d}$ for smooth bounded domains in a smooth Cartan--Hadamard $d$-manifold, $d\ge3$. For relatively compact measurable $E$ of finite perimeter the assertion instead uses the ambient variation $P_g(E)=|D\mathbf1_E|_g(M)$: \[P_g(E)\ge d\omega_d^{1/d}\operatorname{vol}_g(E)^{(d-1)/d}.\] For smooth $\Omega$, $P_g(\Omega)=\operatorname{area}_g(\partial\Omega)$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Sharp fillings in CAT(0)}: Induction reduces a failing inequality to a compact CAT(0) space and an extremizing cycle of small normalized mass. Inward geodesic variations estimate its curvature-like behavior in current charts. The resulting sharp mass comparison contradicts extremality, and Euclidean examples show that the coefficient cannot be improved.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Sharp fillings in CAT(0)}: The paper claims the Euclidean-optimal integral filling inequality for compactly supported integer cycles of dimension at least two in proper CAT(0) spaces. Arbitrary integer multiplicities and ambient dimensions are permitted. The parameters, regularity assumptions and normalization in this question are retained in applying that assertion.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 337 describes Lean coverage for the sharp integral-current CAT(0) filling estimate and its optimal coefficient; the curved-model perimeter theorem is separate.

\textit{Sharp fillings in CAT(0)}: The manuscript links \texttt{FillingCoefficient.lean}, \texttt{SharpCAT0Filling.lean}.

